raw:
{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing what would happen if they uninstalled the game: they would forfeit their in-game purchases and accumulated progress, and they explicitly do not want to let that happen. The request that follows — that the developers add a way to save progress and purchases — targets a cloud-save feature that, by the reviewer's own account, does not currently exist, so under R12 that proposed feature is not codable. But the underlying present design behavior they assert is that progress and purchases are tied to the installation and that exiting (deleting the game) forfeits everything they hold. That is the invested/endowed value structure: accumulated money and time raise the cost of leaving, and the reviewer states the deterrent effect ('I really don't want that to happen'). No search is needed; no other mechanic — monetization conversion, timers, ads, competition, social features — is named or implied.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value indicator: 'Having already spent time and money to improve your status in the game, it's difficult to throw it away.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer has spent money and time in the game and describes the design as one where uninstalling discards all of it; their reluctance to accept that loss shows the accumulated investment operating on the decision to leave, with exit entailing forfeiture of value already held."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is a positive description of the game's features. It names several mechanics in passing: empire building and expansion (\"build and expand my empire\"), real-time player-versus-player combat (\"battling other players in real-time combat\"), and strategy depth. No payment, waiting, grinding, reward, or manipulation mechanic is described — the tone is purely appreciative and no design behavior beyond ordinary gameplay is named. The only pattern the text actually conveys is that the game sets players against each other in live combat, which matches the Competition label regardless of the reviewer's approval.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "Alt. def.: \"The game makes you compete against other players.\"",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly states that the game pits the player against other players in real-time battles, which is competition between players; per R7 and R8 the label is coded from the mechanic even though the reviewer praises it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes hitting a late-game energy bottleneck: at level 113, no further free energy is available ('there is no extra energy'), and the only way to keep making progress is to buy more. The mechanic described is an energy gate on continued advancement with a purchase as the alternative. There is no mention of a timer, regeneration schedule, or appointment the player must keep, so Playing by Appointment is not supported. No currency exchange, grind repetition, or social element is described. The core described design is that progress is contingent on payment.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there is no extra energy. it's getting annoying now, to have any change at progressing you need to purchase.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator: 'The game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that energy has run out at their level and that purchasing is required to have any chance of progressing, making continued advancement contingent on payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a tactical RPG that gates all further play behind an energy resource. The core mechanic being described is a regenerating energy meter: when it is depleted, the player cannot continue making progress, and refilling it takes a long time on a real-world timer. That timer imposes the game's schedule on the player, and because the wait blocks progress rather than merely delaying an optional activity, the mechanic qualifies as dark. Nothing else in the review describes a concrete design behaviour: the comments about limited content, console hopes, and character customization are feature requests or general evaluations, not descriptions of existing manipulative mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play low-level indicator: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits, here energy refilling on a long timer; boundary rule that appointment combined with progress gates is dark",
      "rationale": "The energy meter forces the player to stop and wait on the game's timer before any further progress is possible, making the player play according to the game's refill schedule rather than their own desire."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review splits into two distinct parts. First, the writer (or someone writing under the account) claims that after the player began purchasing Poké Balls, capture attempts began failing more often — \"they keep breaking out\" — so balls are consumed faster and more must be bought. That is an assertion that the game secretly alters the outcome determination (catch odds) based on purchase behaviour to drive further spending; no free alternative or paywall is described, and no currency conversion is named, so the monetary reading is the alleged rigging itself. Second, the closing edit states the review was posted under the account without the account holder's authorship and that its quoted conversations never happened — content published as the player that the player disclaims, which the Friend Spam / Impersonation destination rule treats as impersonation performed through the player's identity. No search is needed: Poké Balls and the capture mechanic are clear from the review and the supplied game name.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule for Alleged rigging: 'Assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts the game secretly lowers capture success once the player buys balls, engineering faster consumption to force more purchases — concealed manipulation of outcomes to encourage continued spending."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Friend Spam / Impersonation, 'Destination of the message' rule: content published as the player — a store review written under their account — assigns the label",
      "rationale": "The account holder states a review was posted under their name that they did not write and whose claims they disavow, implying the game published fabricated content through the player's identity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes two related frustrations: levels that take about a week to pass, and tasks that are effectively impossible without buying boosters. The second clause is a direct statement that in-game progress is blocked unless the player spends money on boosters, which is the paywall arm of Pay to Progress. The first clause reports slow, hard progress but does not describe what the player must repeatedly do to advance (no replaying, farming, or repeated attempts are named), so under the Grinding boundary rule it stays an evaluation of difficulty and time cost rather than a description of repetitive activity. No schedule, timer, or appointment mechanic is described, so no temporal labels apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator: the game reaches a point where further progress is contingent on making a payment",
      "rationale": "The span states that tasks are impossible without buying boosters, meaning progress through those tasks is contingent on payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a limited-time in-game event in MONOPOLY GO! that ran on the game's own clock. The player was three steps from a 525-dice milestone reward, ran out of dice (the resource needed to roll and progress), saw the event still showed '2Days left,' then logged in after sleeping to find the event had been replaced and the nearly completed reward was gone. The harm described is a reward forfeited because the player could not complete the event tasks within the window the game allowed. There is no purchase, no ad, no social mechanic, no daily-login calendar, and no repetitive-task claim in the text; the reviewer's 'waste of time' and 'low blow' are evaluations of that schedule-driven loss. The core design behaviour described is the event deadline operating on the player: progress toward a reward is lost because the game's schedule, not the player's choice, determined when play had to happen.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Fear of missing out: 'Where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.'",
      "rationale": "The event's deadline set when the player could play; rewards were forfeited because the event expired on the game's clock while the player slept, i.e., the player had to complete tasks within the game-defined window or lose the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review lists four separate grievances at the start of a new season: (1) the referral incentive that previously rewarded inviting Facebook friends now pays nothing — a mechanic the reviewer describes as having been removed, not one active in the game, so under R12 it cannot be coded; (2) having accumulated over 500 dice and played at max bet, the reviewer experienced no shutdowns or bank heists — a losing/unlucky run framed as frustration, without any claim that the game conceals or fixes outcomes, so this is not Illusion of Control; (3) sticker packs keep producing duplicates, which in MONOPOLY GO blocks progress toward completing collectible sticker albums; and (4) a login failure attributed to an internet connection error, which is a technical fault suppressed by R11. The sticker complaint carries two dimensions: the random drop mechanism delivering unwanted duplicates (Reward Mania) and the underlying drive to complete the sticker album collection (Complete the Collection). A web search was used to confirm what stickers are for in the game.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "definition (compulsion to acquire all items in a collection)",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is gathering stickers toward an album collection, and the frustration with duplicate drops implies the drive to obtain every unique sticker; the search confirms stickers feed album-completion rewards."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "boundary rule: duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomised pool",
      "rationale": "The span describes the sticker-pack reward delivery as producing repeat duplicates from a randomised pool, sustaining play through the unpredictable draw of the missing items."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "MONOPOLY GO what are stickers and sticker albums used for",
  "search_result": "Stickers in MONOPOLY GO are collectible items used to complete sticker albums, and completing an album grants significant bonus rewards; duplicate stickers do not fill album slots."
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes one continuous design behaviour in the 1v1 lobby of 8 Ball Pool: the interface automatically scrolls to and pre-selects the highest-stakes table the player can afford whenever the game opens or the section is entered. Because the highest bet is already selected by default, an ordinary tap or mis-tap commits the player to a match that can drain all of their coins. The reviewer names the purpose explicitly: to force accidental mis-clicks, strip players of earned coins, and drive them to buy coins with real money. Three distinct mechanics sit in this account. First, an interface whose defaults steer the player toward the most expensive option and make a mis-tap costly — that is attention/selection manipulation by UI design, connected by the reviewer to spending. Second, the outcome that losing all coins compels buying more to keep playing — payment restoring the ability to play is pay-to-continue. Third, the purchase itself: coins are the object bought with real money, which is the real-money-to-currency conversion. The 'Default Table Lock' request is a proposed fix for the existing behaviour and does not itself add a label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins. This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks so players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "definition (interface contains elements designed to focus the player on specific options) and the vs UI complaint boundary: the reviewer attributes the design to a purpose it serves against the player's intent ('trick to force accidental mis-clicks') and ties it to spending",
      "rationale": "The interface pre-selects and centres the most expensive table so a routine tap becomes an unintended high-stakes wager; the span explicitly frames this as a deliberate dark-UX device whose payoff is a coerced real-money purchase, satisfying UI Misdirection's purchase-bearing arm."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall (Alt. def.: the game becomes impossible to continue playing without payment)",
      "rationale": "Losing all coins through the rigged default leaves the player unable to keep playing at their level, and the only way back is buying coins with real money, i.e., continued play becomes contingent on payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency / definition: the currency is the object acquired with real-world money; boundary rule notes 'buy coins' shows the conversion even when money is not named",
      "rationale": "'Real ones' refers to coins purchased with real money, describing the exchange of real currency for the in-game coin currency — the conversion relation that defines Intermediate Currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes the game's economy and fairness after reaching a high level. First, they assert the randomness is rigged against the player — a claim about how outcomes are determined, not merely a bad run. Second, they state that playing without paying real money is not rewarding, and they support this with an economic detail: entering a level costs 2200 (an in-game coin stake) while winning barely returns that amount, so the player's funds drain and continued rewarding play effectively requires spending money. The final line that rewards need a revamp is a complaint about reward value, not about a reward-delivery mechanism, so it does not code Reward Mania. No currency purchase with real money is described, so no Intermediate Currency tag.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule under Illusion of Control: assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes (a non-random deal, a fixed win ratio, a rigged schedule)",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts that the game's randomness is deliberately set against the player — an allegation that outcomes are manipulated rather than a report of mere bad luck."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money.. at around level 100 it costs 2200 but if you win the level you'll barely make that back",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress, 'Free alternative' boundary rule: phrases like 'without paying' imply a paid alternative, and the tag applies when the free route falls short of smooth advancement",
      "rationale": "Playing without paying is described as unrewarding, and the economy detail (entry cost of 2200 exceeding what a win returns) shows the free route drains the player's funds, making payment the dependable way to keep progressing rewardingly."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a base-builder war game across three mechanics. First, they characterise the game itself as an unoriginal 'grinder', meaning progress runs on repetitive task play rather than design ingenuity. Second, they describe the event system: after weeks of play it became clear that a general (non-spending) player cannot win an event, and that winning is determined by money rather than skill — 'zero skill pay to play, zero skill pay to win' is a pay-to-win formulation, and the $30k figure for the last event shows victory priced, not earned. Third, that account implies events pit players against each other in competition where big spenders dominate and ordinary players are locked out. The closing sentence about serious gamers spending money is a plea about other players' conduct, not a game mechanic, so it codes nothing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Same old same old grinder.",
      "rule_applied": "Alt. def. 'Being compelled to complete monotonous tasks in order to make progress'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names the game a 'grinder', describing a design whose progress model is repetitive grinding rather than anything inventive."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event. Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win and Pay Wall low-level indicators of Pay to Progress",
      "rationale": "Event victory is closed to non-paying players ('no opportunity for a general player') and outcomes are decided by spending rather than skill ('zero skill pay to win'), both forms of paying to progress/win."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'Patterns that make players against each other in competition'",
      "rationale": "Winning events implies ranked competition among players; the review describes that competition as dominated by spenders (with the last event costing 30k to win), so ordinary players are pitted against paying rivals."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
