{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a bingo collection loop. They play rounds repeatedly to collect 'photos' (collectible items) and report that goals are not reachable in reasonable time. The most concrete mechanic: after every round they lose, the game presents a purchase prompt offering to spend extra coins to claim a missing photo needed to complete the set — a buying prompt that intercepts play at the moment of failure. The central grievance is that when they pay for that offer, the promised photo is never delivered ('I GET 2-3 BINGOS BUT STILL DONT GET THE OFFER/PHOTO I WAS PROMISED'), a repeated misleading offer rather than a one-off fault. The reviewer finally quits, having spent money. The mechanics named or implied: repeated rounds as the path to collectibles (grinding loop), set-completion as the goal the offers exploit (collection drive), an offer prompt interrupting sessions after losses (prompted purchase), and offer copy that promises a deliverable it does not provide (deceitful question/offer wording).",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I play round after round to collect 'photos'",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'Performing repetitive and tedious tasks' in order to make progress; boundary rule requiring the span to name the repeated activity",
      "rationale": "'Round after round' names the repeated activity the player must run to acquire photos and advance the collection, supported by the complaint that goals are not reachable 'within a decent amount of time'."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "claim a photo I don't have to complete the set",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: 'the compulsion to acquire all the items... in a game'; collection-completion framing",
      "rationale": "The game dangles a specific unowned photo needed to finish the set, and the reviewer's play and spending are both directed at completing that collection, which is the mechanism the offers exploit."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "after each round that I lose it will ask me if I want to spend extra coins to claim a photo I don't have to complete the set",
      "rule_applied": "Prompted to Buy (low-level) and boundary rule: 'a buying prompt directly intercepts gameplay... offers that interrupt a session'",
      "rationale": "A purchase offer is presented automatically after every lost round, intercepting play at the moment of failure and repeated throughout the session, which is the prompted-purchase friction reduction."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_AestheticManipulation",
      "span": "I'll spend coins on said offer, I GET 2-3 BINGOS BUT STILL DONT GET THE OFFER/PHOTO I WAS PROMISED",
      "rule_applied": "Alt. def.: 'using misleading questions... to influence player behaviour'; boundary rule placing deception in what the interface/offer says in Aesthetic Manipulation",
      "rationale": "The offer's wording promises the missing photo in exchange for coins, but payment repeatedly delivers something else; the deception is carried by what the offer text says versus what the player receives."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

raw:
{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes the mid-game loop in Last War: repeatedly gathering resources while defending against raids, all to gain levels that unlock more attempts at a side mini-game. The word 'grinding' names the repetitive resource-collection task directly and ties it to progression ('so you can level up'), which is Grinding. The sentence about bigger alliances attacking smaller ones and taking their resources describes an asymmetric player-versus-player structure: stronger groups are rewarded with the defender's resources for raiding weaker groups. That matches the Encourages Anti-Social Behavior boundary rule about stronger players rewarded for attacking weaker ones, not merely ordinary sanctioned raid competition. The vague comment about advertisement becoming 'not that great' describes no forced or rewarded ad mechanic and codes nothing. The closing statement of dedication and disappointment reports time invested and dissatisfaction, but shows no mechanism raising the cost of leaving, so it does not reach Easy to Get, Hard to Lose. No currency, timers, daily incentives, or payment are described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "It's just grinding resources trying not to get raided so you can level up",
      "rule_applied": "definition: 'Performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names the repetitive task ('grinding resources') and links it to advancement ('so you can level up'), describing the activity required for progress rather than just slow progress in the abstract."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior",
      "span": "The bigger alliances all just attack the smaller alliances and take their resources",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Permitted harm: assign where the span shows a reward flowing to the player who acts harmfully; and asymmetric-conduct rule: 'stronger players rewarded for attacking weaker ones'",
      "rationale": "The design lets larger alliances raid smaller ones and directly take their resources, so the harmful act and the aggressor's gain appear in the same span, with the asymmetry between strong and weak made explicit."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is talking about the sticker album collection mechanic in MONOPOLY GO!. Sticker packs come from a randomised reward pool and produce duplicate stickers, which the reviewer experiences as the reason the collection stays incomplete. The game is described as blocking completion of the full set of albums unless the player pays. Three mechanics sit in this short text: a random delivery system whose duplicates deny the desired items, a collection completion goal the player is being pulled toward, and a pay gate placed on finishing that collection.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "always get duplicates",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator; vs Reward described only by its value boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The span describes how rewards are delivered from a randomised pool, with repeated unwanted duplicate drops rather than the items wanted, which is the delivery-schedule mechanism Reward Mania covers."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "never lets you finish all the sets",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is pursuing the full sticker album sets, and the game is framed as withholding completion of the collection, capitalising on the drive to collect all items."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "never lets you finish all the sets unless you pay",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator",
      "rationale": "Finishing the sticker sets is stated to be contingent on payment, so further progress in the collection is gated behind spending money."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a progress wall: the reviewer has been stuck on the same level for two months and is considering quitting. They then state they refuse to pay for power-ups, which implies the game offers paid power-ups as a way to get past exactly these hard levels. The final sentence wishes daily login rewards existed to help with harder levels, which is a request for a feature not currently present, so per R12 it does not code as a daily-reward mechanic. No timers, grinding activity, or other described mechanics appear.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "stuck on level 195 for almost 2 months. getting ready to uninstall it. I refuse to pay for power ups.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Pay to Skip indicator; vs Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "Being stuck on a level for two months with paid power-ups offered as the way past it shows further progress is gated behind payment; the reviewer declining to pay does not remove the design, per R7."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer lists complaints about RAID: Shadow Legends. Missions not crediting appears to be a tracking fault, which the codebook treats as a technical malfunction rather than a designed pattern. The lack of in-game instructions is a documentation gap, not a listed dark pattern. 'You almost have to buy stuff just to be able to keep playing' states that continued play is contingent on spending, which matches a pay wall. 'The amount of grinding you do in comparison to what you get is practically nothing' names the repetitive-task activity itself and contrasts the effort with the meagre payout, which matches Grinding. The 'pay to unequip' complaint describes a fee on a gear-management action; nothing ties that fee to progression, real-money conversion, stranded value, or an interface trick, and it resembles a quality-of-life restriction rather than a progress barrier, so no label maps onto it. 'Shards and gems are too hard to get' complains about earning rates of currencies but does not describe real money being converted into them, nor a wager, so Intermediate Currency and Gambling are not established.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "you almost have to buy stuff just to be able to keep playing",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator: 'The game becomes impossible to continue playing without payment'",
      "rationale": "Continued play is described as nearly contingent on making purchases, i.e., payment gates the ability to keep playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "the amount and of grinding you do in comparison to what you get is practically nothing",
      "rule_applied": "definition (performing repetitive tasks to make progress), alt. def. 'compelled to complete monotonous tasks in order to make progress'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names the grind activity outright and measures the effort invested against the negligible reward, describing repetitive play required to obtain anything in the game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer lists six separate grievances, all about purchases and timers. #1 describes being charged 10 gold pieces for five extra moves at a moment when they still had moves left (2 left, or 7 moves with only 1 used): the charge is framed as unwanted and automatic, implying an accidental/unconfirmed purchase rather than a deliberate buy. #2 says a paid package with unlimited lives delivered everything except the lives: this is a failed delivery, with no design mechanic described, so nothing is coded from it. #3 and #6 describe purchased or earned bonuses and packages whose real-time clocks keep ticking while the player is not playing, so their value erodes overnight on the game's schedule. #4 says free gold winnings are insufficient to keep playing, so the player must buy gold: this shows a premium currency purchasable with real money standing between the player and continued play. #5 is a pure price complaint with no mechanic and codes nothing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "I was charged 10 gold pcs for 5 moves, I had 2 moves left. I had 7 moves but only used 1 move.",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases low-level pattern: 'Making it easy to accidentally spend money with no confirmation or ability to undo or refund the action.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer was charged for extra moves despite still having moves available, describing a deduction they did not intend or need, with no confirmation or reversal mentioned."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "You cannot win enough gold to play without buying sometimes",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency low-level pattern: real-world money is used to acquire the in-game currency 'gold', whose price and value are opaque.",
      "rationale": "Gold pieces are the in-game premium currency; the contrast between winning it and 'buying' it implies the currency is acquired with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You cannot win enough gold to play without buying sometimes",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall low-level pattern: the game reaches a point where continued play is contingent on making a payment; free alternative falls short of smooth advancement.",
      "rationale": "Free gold winnings are insufficient, so the player must purchase gold to keep playing, making payment the route to continued progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The timed bonuses tick away even though I am not playing. I go to bed with bonuses to wake up with them almost gone or gone.",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Fear of missing out: 'Where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.'",
      "rationale": "Bonuses have a real-time countdown set by the game that runs whether or not the player is playing, so using them requires playing on the game's clock and the reviewer describes the loss that follows from not doing so."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review's substance is a failed attempt to finish a card album. The reviewer was two cards short of the grand prize awarded for completing the album, so they spent real money on a deal that was presented as delivering NEW cards; instead the deal returned duplicate cards, which are useless toward completing the album. The mechanics this reveals: an album-completion goal that collects cards toward a grand prize (Complete the Collection), and card deals bought with real money whose contents are drawn from a randomized pool, producing duplicates rather than the missing pieces (a chance-based paid reward). The 'Free Cash' promotion is external marketing and outside the app, so it is not coded. The scam complaint is an evaluation of the purchase outcome, not itself a mechanic, but the underlying design it describes — paid card packs with random, duplicating contents blocking the final collection goal — is what the labels capture.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "I had two cards left which would grant me the grand prize for completing an album",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is positioned two items short of completing an album set, and the duplicate cards keep the collection unfinished, showing a collection-completion structure driving the player's spending."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "i bought a deal which promised i would get NEW cards, and all i got were useless duplicates!",
      "rule_applied": "Loot Boxes: 'Patterns that encourages players to spend money on chance-based rewards.'",
      "rationale": "Real money is spent on a card deal whose contents are drawn at random, evidenced by the reviewer receiving useless duplicates instead of the specific missing cards they needed."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "all i got were useless duplicates!",
      "rule_applied": "Reward Mania vs Reward described only by its value: duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomised pool",
      "rationale": "The complaint describes the delivery of duplicate drops from a randomized card pool, the variable-reward delivery mechanism that sustains continued engagement rather than the reward's value."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

