raw:
{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing the game's long-term progression economy. They name several things at once: rewards shrinking with each patch; the meta shifting so that only newly released characters can still deal damage, which forces players to keep pulling new characters; and the game becoming pay to win. 'Pull' is Genshin's gacha mechanic — the chance-based draw that delivers characters — and the reviewer treats pulling as mandatory for staying competitive, which means older owned characters lose their damage value over time. The art-quality complaint about the archon quest background is an evaluation of production quality, not a game mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "forcing us to pull for new characters if we want to actually do damage. the game is getting more and more pay 2 win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win low-level indicator: 'A player can spend real money to purchase something that gives them an advantage in the game.'",
      "rationale": "Acquiring new characters through pulls is presented as the requirement for dealing damage, and the reviewer explicitly frames the game as increasingly pay to win, i.e., spending money buys the competitive advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "the Meta changes more and more every single day basically forcing us to pull for new characters if we want to actually do damage",
      "rule_applied": "Definition / Power Creep boundary rule: devaluation carried by the recency qualifier attached to a capability requirement.",
      "rationale": "The ever-shifting meta means only newly pulled characters can still deal damage, asserting that older purchased characters lose their value over time through new releases, which drives further pulls."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "forcing us to pull for new characters if we want to actually do damage. the game is getting more and more pay 2 win",
      "rule_applied": "Loot Boxes low-level indicator and the Gambling vs Reward Mania rule (chance mechanic plus explicit monetary framing present in the span).",
      "rationale": "Pulling characters is a chance-based gacha draw whose outcome is not guaranteed, and the pay-to-win framing places real money behind those draws, making this spending on luck-based acquisition of prizes."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "forcing us to pull for new characters",
      "rule_applied": "Reward Mania indicator: 'Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners.'",
      "rationale": "The span names the gacha pull mechanic as the game's mandated route for obtaining new characters; naming pulls instantiates the Reward Mania pattern regardless of the reviewer's disapproval, and no monetary stake needs to be stated for this label."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing the game's real content versus its advertised pitch. The first grievance, that the game is not what was advertised and is instead a base-growth simulator with many microtransaction menus, refers to external marketing and the store listing, which R13 places outside the unit; the bare presence of many purchase menus is not described through any friction-reducing mechanism, so no Monetary label attaches to it. The second grievance is in-game design: after a point the game encourages player-versus-player attacks, and the reviewer states there is a large gap between paying and non-paying players. That describes a competitive structure in which money buys an advantage, which is the Pay to Win form of Pay to Progress, and the attack-on-attack PvP structure is ordinary competitive design, so Competition rather than Encourages Anti-Social Behavior, whose boundary rule assigns raids and sanctioned attacks between players to Competition.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there's a huge gap between p2w and f2p",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: “Patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money.”",
      "rationale": "Naming the p2w/f2p gap asserts that spending money confers a decisive advantage over non-paying players in the game's conflicts, i.e., progress and standing are obtainable through payment."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "players are encouraged to attack each other",
      "rule_applied": "Alt. def.: “The game makes you compete against other players.”",
      "rationale": "The design actively directs players into player-versus-player attacks, which is competition between players; per the Encourages Anti-Social boundary rule, sanctioned attacking of other players is ordinary competitive design and codes Competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is complaining about a recent update to a strategy game. The review names three design facts: the developer removed the 'open silver' feature and any player-vs-player gameplay; growth in the game is described as requiring spending; and money already spent on growth has been rendered pointless by the update. The PvP and silver complaints describe features that were removed — i.e., mechanics no longer present — and the closing demand to 'bring back open silver' is a request for restoration, which R12 excludes. 'Boring farmville' is a quality characterization of the remaining content rather than a description of a repetitive task gating progress, so it does not meet Grinding. The statement that spending is required for meaningful growth describes a current, functioning design rule and is the one codable mechanic in the review. The devaluation of past spending through removal of PvP is closer to a sunk-cost grievance about a discontinued mode than to Power Creep, which requires purchased items losing value through newer content in order to drive new purchases; nothing here suggests the update pushes new purchases.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You have to spend to have any meaningful growth",
      "rule_applied": "definition / Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that meaningful in-game growth is contingent on spending money, i.e., progress without payment falls short, which is a pay-to-progress design rule currently in effect."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}
